    "summary": "Third set rockets with China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider, Estimated Prophet & Terrapin Station",
    "blurb": "The highlight of the night is a high‑energy third set that bursts from China Cat Sunflower into I Know You Rider, then rolls through Estimated Prophet and a soaring Terrapin Station. Althea and Candyman shine in the second set while the first delivers solid classics like Ripple.",
    "review": "On October 29, 1980 at Radio City Music Hall the Dead served up a show that’s remembered most for its blistering third set. The transition from China Cat Sunflower into I Know You Rider is tight and exuberant, leading straight into an inspired Estimated Prophet and a majestic Terrapin Station that showcases Brent’s synth work and Phil’s bass groove. Earlier in the evening Althea receives fan‑fanned praise, with Jerry’s tone sparkling and Bob adding a spirited solo, while Candyman and The Wheel keep the momentum rolling through Set 2. The first set may not have been earth‑shattering—Ripple and Dire Wolf sit comfortably—but it provides a solid foundation for the fireworks that follow. Recording wise, the audience capture by Jim Wise offers good dynamics despite occasional muddiness, and the best‑available soundboard (the Beyer/Stankiewicz dump) is clean enough to let the band’s nuances shine.\n\nOverall, the performance balances tight musicianship with the loose, exploratory vibe Deadheads love. The show isn’t flawless—some listeners note pitch/hiss issues on certain SBD sources and a few weaker moments in Set 2—but the high points more than compensate, making this a solid entry for anyone looking to hear late‑‘80s Dead at their most adventurous.",
